Article 778. Maintenance records
1. Aircraft operators must establish procedures and systems for maintaining aircraft maintenance records, including records for maintaining continuous airworthiness standards and the aircraft's Technical Log.
2. Documents for maintaining continuous airworthiness standards of aircraft include:
a) Total operating time (hours, scheduled time and number of takeoffs and landings) of aircraft and life-long equipment (LLP). In case the LLP's records are lost or destroyed, the aircraft operator must use other records to evaluate the safety condition. If it cannot be determined, they must not continue to use them, and immediately report to the Vietnam Aviation Administration and urgently search for replacement records to determine the current status of the equipment;
b) Status of compliance with airworthiness directives and other mandatory requirements for maintaining airworthiness;
c) Information on design changes and repairs made to aircraft or aircraft equipment;
d) Operating time (hours, scheduled time and number of takeoffs and landings) since the last maintenance of the aircraft or equipment with mandatory maintenance periods;
d) Status of compliance with the aircraft's maintenance program (including a list of maintenance tasks that have been or are being delayed compared to the maintenance program);
e) Report on weighing and balancing the aircraft's load;
g) Information related to equipment installed on the aircraft, engines, propellers and life-saving equipment must be managed and full and detailed information including equipment specification numbers can be retrieved; type, registration number, factory number of the aircraft on which the equipment is installed and the time of disassembly and installation of the equipment on the aircraft; total hours and operating cycles or remaining life of the equipment;
h) Detailed maintenance records showing that post-maintenance clearance requirements have been complied with.
3. The aircraft's technical logbook with full information as prescribed in Article 776 of these Regulations.
4. Certificates permitting the operation of aircraft engines, aircraft propellers, aircraft equipment and formats are specified in Article 775 of these Regulations.
5. Aircraft operators must retain records and documents specified in Clauses 2 and 3 of this Article for the following period:
a) At least 12 months from the time the aircraft and related equipment mentioned in Points a, b, c, d, dd, e and g, Clauses 2 and 3 of this Article are no longer in use;
b) At least 12 months from the date of signing confirmation of maintenance completion for the contents specified in Point h, Clause 2 of this Article;
c) 2 years for aircraft technical logbooks;
6. In case the aircraft is temporarily transferred to another aircraft operator, the aircraft operator must ensure that the documents specified in Clause 2 of this Article are provided to the receiving operator.
7. In case an aircraft is permanently transferred from one aircraft operator to another, the aircraft operator must ensure that the documents specified in Clause 2 of this Article are transferred to the receiving operator."},"translatedText":"Article 778. Maintenance records

