2. Aircraft operators must ensure that all flight crew members and ground personnel are trained to enforce applicable restrictions regarding the use of portable electronic devices in accordance with these procedures.
3. Aircraft operators regulate the types of portable electronic devices allowed to be used during each phase of the flight, except that medical devices necessary to support passengers' physiological functions can be used at all times and do not need to be turned off.
4. Aircraft operators must establish procedures related to the storage of portable electronic devices, including:
a) Identify the stages of flight in which portable electronic devices must be stored;
b) Determine suitable storage locations, taking into account the size and weight of the portable electronic device.
5. Aircraft operators must provide passengers with general information about the use of portable electronic devices before the flight, including:
a) what types of portable electronic devices are allowed during each phase of the flight;
b) when and where portable electronic devices must be stored;
c) Passengers must always follow the flight crew's instructions.
6. Regulations on the use of portable electronic devices must be part of the safety instructions for passengers. Operators must remind passengers to pay attention and avoid distractions during these instructions.
7. When a power supply is equipped at the seat for passengers to use, the following regulations must apply:
a) Must provide passengers with safety instructions;
b) Portable electronic devices must be disconnected from all power sources at the seat during taxiing, takeoff, approach, landing and in unusual or emergency situations;
c) The flight crew, flight attendants and technical crew members must be aware of the appropriate means to turn off the power supply at seats used for portable electronic devices.
8. Safety measures of operators during boarding and in all stages of the flight:
a) Establish appropriate coordination between flight crew, flight attendants and technical team to handle interference or other safety issues related to portable electronic devices;
b) Predict that harmful devices must be turned off;
c) Properly handle the loss of thermal control of batteries, especially lithium batteries, and the risk of fire that may arise according to ICAO Technical Guidance Documents.
d) The aircraft commander may request that portable electronic devices be decommissioned and stored.
d) When aircraft operators restrict the use of portable electronic devices, consideration should be given to handling special requirements for operating T-PEDs during any phase of the flight for specific reasons.
9. Suspected or confirmed incidents of interference must be reported to the Vietnam Aviation Administration. Where applicable, to assist in technical surveillance and investigation, the report should describe the suspected device, identify the make and model number, the location of the device on the aircraft at the time of the incident, indications of interference, contact information for the person using the device, and the results of the measures taken by the flight crew.
