7. Quy trình khai thác, chương trình huấn luyện chuyển đổi chéo, nội dung ghi nhận chéo, giới hạn vận hành nhiều loại hoặc biến thể tàu bay và yêu cầu duy trì kinh nghiệm hiện tại được quy định trong chương trình huấn luyện và tài liệu hướng dẫn khai thác của người khai thác tàu bay.</p>","translations":{"en":"Article 847. Operations on more than one type of aircraft

Article 847. Operations on more than one type of aircraft
1. For aircraft, aircraft pilots are allowed to perform tasks, train, test, evaluate, maintain competence and experience simultaneously on more than one type of aircraft when meeting the following regulations:
a) Documents of the aircraft manufacturer, data suitable for operation or technical documents issued by the aircraft design organization containing regulations and instructions on operation, training, inspection, cross-recording and maintenance of concurrent capacity for those types of aircraft.
b. Comply with regulations on training, testing, maintaining capacity, and operations developed by the aircraft operator in the document system according to the aircraft manufacturer's documents, operational appropriate data or technical documents issued by the aircraft design organization.
2. For single-engine helicopters, aircraft operators are allowed to group single-engine helicopters to organize operations, train and test the capabilities of aircraft pilots when the following requirements are met:
a) The helicopter group includes only single-engine turbine helicopters operated under visual flight rules or only includes single-engine piston helicopters operated under visual flight rules;
b) The pilot has completed at least 02 competency tests on the same type of helicopter within the 3-year period before operating more than one type of helicopter in the same group;
c) The training program and operating manual stipulate the list of helicopter types in the same group, the content of training, testing, maintaining current experience, the conditions for operating more than one type of helicopter on the same day, the time for briefing or self-study before switching to another type or type of helicopter, and the number of types or types of helicopters operated on the same day.
3. For multi-engine helicopters, the pilot is assigned to operate more than one type of helicopter when all of the following requirements are met:
a) The aircraft pilot has a license, rating and has completed the training, inspection and maintenance requirements for each type of helicopter assigned to operate, unless the content of cross-recording is determined in operational data or technical documents issued by the aircraft design organization;
b) For helicopters with a maximum takeoff weight of more than 5,700 kg or an operational passenger seat configuration of more than 19 seats, the pilot of the aircraft must not operate more than two types of helicopters, except in cases where operational data or technical documents issued by the aircraft design organization provide for cross-recording of requirements for training, testing and maintaining current experience;
c) For multi-engine helicopters other than those specified in Point b of this Clause, the pilot shall not operate more than three types or groups of helicopter types, unless operationally relevant data or technical documents issued by the aircraft design organization provide otherwise;
d) The aircraft operator's training program stipulates the content of training, testing, cross-recording, maintaining current experience and operating limits for each type of helicopter;
d) Operational manual documents specifying conditions for operating more than one type of helicopter, conditions for converting between types of helicopters, time for training or self-study before switching to another type of helicopter, number of types of helicopters to be operated on the same day or on the same flight shift and applicable operating limits.
4. For specialized operations, training and inspection content related to specialized operations tasks and not dependent on the type or group of aircraft types is cross-recorded between types or groups of aircraft types on the basis of the safety risk assessment of the aircraft operator and specified in the training program or operating manual of the aircraft operator.
5. Periodic flight operational inspections are arranged alternately between types or variants of aircraft when that type or type of aircraft has similar operating characteristics, operating environment, operating methods and operating conditions; Alternate content is determined in the operational suitability data or technical documents issued by the aircraft design organization or specified in the aircraft operator's operating manual on the basis of assessment of operational similarity.
6. For helicopter operations using equipment, cross-recording of in-flight operational checks between types or variants of helicopters is performed when the content of cross-recording is determined in the appropriate operational data or technical documents issued by the aircraft design organization.
7. Operational procedures, cross-conversion training programs, cross-recognition content, limitations on operating multiple types or variants of aircraft and requirements for maintaining current experience are specified in the training program and operating manuals of the aircraft operator."},"translatedText":"Article 847. Operations on more than one type of aircraft

