Article 879. Contents of periodic competency testing for aircraft pilots
1. Checking the aircraft operator's competency for the aircraft is carried out in the standard crew composition and, when applicable, includes the following operations in the pilot's role:
a) Abort takeoff on appropriate flight simulator; In case of flying on an airplane, only simulate operations and procedures;
b) Takeoff with an engine failure between V1 and V2 or, if taken in an aircraft, at a safe speed above V2;
c) 3D approach to minimum altitude, in the case of multi-engine aircraft, with one engine inoperative;
d) 2D approach to minimum altitude;
d) At least one of the 3D or 2D approaches is an RNP APCH or RNP AR APCH approach as applicable;
e) Instrument reentry from minimum altitude, in the case of multi-engine aircraft, with one engine inoperative;
g) Landing with one engine inoperative; for single-engine aircraft, perform a mandatory landing practice check;
h) Abnormal and emergency situations appropriate to the type of aircraft;
i) Evaluate the capacity of flight crew members to manage flight crew resources.
2. Check the helicopter operator's ability to comply with the following requirements:
a) The inspection program on aircraft or simulated flight training equipment is developed to ensure that major failures of aircraft systems and related processes are inspected within a cycle of no more than 03 years;
b) The aircraft operator determines the major failure of the aircraft system to test the capacity of the aircraft operator on the basis of risk assessment, taking into account warnings or recommendations related to the failure, the severity of the situation or failure, the results of the handling process including immediate landing or the earliest possible landing instead of landing when convenient, documents of the organization responsible for designing the aircraft type when available and a list of abnormal and emergency procedures;
c) For single-engine helicopters, each aircraft operator's competency test includes engine failure, directional control failure or malfunction, and hydraulic system failure when applicable;
d) Where a group of types of single-engine turboprop or single-engine piston helicopters is determined to extend the validity of the aircraft operator's qualification test, all major aircraft system failures are inspected on each type within a period not to exceed 03 years, unless credits related to training, testing and the requirement to maintain current experience are specified in the operational suitability data or technical documents issued by the aircraft design organization for the type or variant thereof. mandarin;
d) For pilots holding an instrument rating, the aircraft operator's competency test includes the following additional normal, abnormal or emergency procedures: 3D approach to minimum altitude, instrument reentry, 2D approach to minimum altitude, at least one of the 3D or 2D approaches is an RNP APCH or RNP AR APCH approach when applicable, simulation of single engine failure during a 3D approach or 2D to minimum altitude for multi-engine helicopters, and approach with failure of flight control system, flight command system, flight equipment or navigation equipment as applicable;
e) Before a flight crew member without a valid instrument rating is assigned to operate VMC at night, the flight crew member completes a night rating check; then a second competency test is performed at night;
g) Checking the capacity of the aircraft operator is carried out with a qualified aircraft pilot for multi-pilot operations and a qualified aircraft pilot for single-pilot operations;
h) Aircraft pilots who simultaneously operate a multi-member flight crew and operate a single pilot are tested for competency in a multi-member flight crew; At the same time, failures or essential operations as a pilot are checked, including at least two unusual or emergency operations related to the aircraft type on the basis of risk assessment, and instrument approach, reentry and troubleshooting for operations under instrument flight rules;
i) Flight crew members are assessed for their skills in managing flight crew resources according to the method specified in the operational manual;
k) In case simulator flight training equipment for aircraft is not available, the aircraft operator is allowed to carry out on-board inspection when there are measures to minimize risks to maintain an appropriate level of safety. In case of major failure of the aircraft system that cannot be fully practiced on the aircraft due to related risks or environmental conditions, the test content is carried out in the form of a partial simulation according to pre-prepared measures, risk assessment and ensuring that the aircraft can be immediately restored to normal operating status;
l) The test includes abnormal and emergency situations appropriate to the aircraft type;
m) The test includes an assessment of the flight crew member's ability to manage flight crew resources.
3. The inspection content specified in Clauses 1 and 2 of this Article is performed by flight examiners. Aircraft operators whose test results are recognized to issue airline transport pilot licenses, type ratings, and extend the validity of aircraft type ratings replace the aircraft operator's competency test on the basis of conformity assessment. The content of accreditation is specified in the aircraft operator's documentation system.
4. Checking emergency and safety equipment is performed periodically for those who have been trained on emergency, safety and survival equipment according to the periodic training program.
5. Flight crew members complete a 12-month routine in-flight operational check. In-flight operational inspection complies with the following requirements:
a) The aircraft operator performs an operational check on the flight path to evaluate the ability of flight crew members to fully and safely perform an operational flight according to the standard operating procedures specified in the operating manual;
b) Checking flight operations includes implementing pre-flight, in-flight and post-flight procedures; use operating equipment equipped on aircraft; perform the duties of driver and supervisor; Coordinate flight crew, make operational decisions and manage flight crew resources;
c) The test flight path is appropriate and representative of the aircraft pilot's normal operating environment;
d) Aircraft pilots assigned to perform the duties of pilot and supervisor are tested in both functions, except in the case of single-pilot operations;
d) Checking operations on the flight path is carried out by the aircraft commander designated by the aircraft operator according to the standards, conditions and procedures specified in the training program and operational guidance documents;
e) For single-pilot operations, en-route operations checks are performed in an environment representative of the actual operating conditions of single-pilot operations;
g) Contents of in-flight operations inspection on aircraft periodically every 12 months are specified in Appendix LII attached to these Regulations."},"translatedText":"Article 879. Contents of periodic competency testing for aircraft pilots

Article 879. Contents of periodic competency testing for aircraft pilots
1. Checking the aircraft operator's competency for the aircraft is carried out in the standard crew composition and, when applicable, includes the following operations in the pilot's role:
a) Abort takeoff on appropriate flight simulator; In case of flying on an airplane, only simulate operations and procedures;
b) Takeoff with an engine failure between V1 and V2 or, if taken in an aircraft, at a safe speed above V2;
c) 3D approach to minimum altitude, in the case of multi-engine aircraft, with one engine inoperative;
d) 2D approach to minimum altitude;
d) At least one of the 3D or 2D approaches is an RNP APCH or RNP AR APCH approach as applicable;
e) Instrument reentry from minimum altitude, in the case of multi-engine aircraft, with one engine inoperative;
g) Landing with one engine inoperative; for single-engine aircraft, perform a mandatory landing practice check;
h) Abnormal and emergency situations appropriate to the type of aircraft;
i) Evaluate the capacity of flight crew members to manage flight crew resources.
2. Check the helicopter operator's ability to comply with the following requirements:
a) The inspection program on aircraft or simulated flight training equipment is developed to ensure that major failures of aircraft systems and related processes are inspected within a cycle of no more than 03 years;
b) The aircraft operator determines the major failure of the aircraft system to test the capacity of the aircraft operator on the basis of risk assessment, taking into account warnings or recommendations related to the failure, the severity of the situation or failure, the results of the handling process including immediate landing or the earliest possible landing instead of landing when convenient, documents of the organization responsible for designing the aircraft type when available and a list of abnormal and emergency procedures;
c) For single-engine helicopters, each aircraft operator's competency test includes engine failure, directional control failure or malfunction, and hydraulic system failure when applicable;
d) Where a group of types of single-engine turboprop or single-engine piston helicopters is determined to extend the validity of the aircraft operator's qualification test, all major aircraft system failures are inspected on each type within a period not to exceed 03 years, unless credits related to training, testing and the requirement to maintain current experience are specified in the operational suitability data or technical documents issued by the aircraft design organization for the type or variant thereof. mandarin;
d) For pilots holding an instrument rating, the aircraft operator's competency test includes the following additional normal, abnormal or emergency procedures: 3D approach to minimum altitude, instrument reentry, 2D approach to minimum altitude, at least one of the 3D or 2D approaches is an RNP APCH or RNP AR APCH approach when applicable, simulation of single engine failure during a 3D approach or 2D to minimum altitude for multi-engine helicopters, and approach with failure of flight control system, flight command system, flight equipment or navigation equipment as applicable;
e) Before a flight crew member without a valid instrument rating is assigned to operate VMC at night, the flight crew member completes a night rating check; then a second competency test is performed at night;
g) Checking the capacity of the aircraft operator is carried out with a qualified aircraft pilot for multi-pilot operations and a qualified aircraft pilot for single-pilot operations;
h) Aircraft pilots who simultaneously operate a multi-member flight crew and operate a single pilot are tested for competency in a multi-member flight crew; At the same time, failures or essential operations as a pilot are checked, including at least two unusual or emergency operations related to the aircraft type on the basis of risk assessment, and instrument approach, reentry and troubleshooting for operations under instrument flight rules;
i) Flight crew members are assessed for their skills in managing flight crew resources according to the method specified in the operational manual;
k) In case simulator flight training equipment for aircraft is not available, the aircraft operator is allowed to carry out on-board inspection when there are measures to minimize risks to maintain an appropriate level of safety. In case of major failure of the aircraft system that cannot be fully practiced on the aircraft due to related risks or environmental conditions, the test content is carried out in the form of a partial simulation according to pre-prepared measures, risk assessment and ensuring that the aircraft can be immediately restored to normal operating status;
l) The test includes abnormal and emergency situations appropriate to the aircraft type;
m) The test includes an assessment of the flight crew member's ability to manage flight crew resources.
3. The inspection content specified in Clauses 1 and 2 of this Article is performed by flight examiners. Aircraft operators whose test results are recognized to issue airline transport pilot licenses, type ratings, and extend the validity of aircraft type ratings replace the aircraft operator's competency test on the basis of conformity assessment. The content of accreditation is specified in the aircraft operator's documentation system.
4. Checking emergency and safety equipment is performed periodically for those who have been trained on emergency, safety and survival equipment according to the periodic training program.
5. Flight crew members complete a 12-month routine in-flight operational check. In-flight operational inspection complies with the following requirements:
a) The aircraft operator performs an operational check on the flight path to evaluate the ability of flight crew members to fully and safely perform an operational flight according to the standard operating procedures specified in the operating manual;
b) Checking flight operations includes implementing pre-flight, in-flight and post-flight procedures; use operating equipment equipped on aircraft; perform the duties of driver and supervisor; Coordinate flight crew, make operational decisions and manage flight crew resources;
c) The test flight path is appropriate and representative of the aircraft pilot's normal operating environment;
d) Aircraft pilots assigned to perform the duties of pilot and supervisor are tested in both functions, except in the case of single-pilot operations;
d) Checking operations on the flight path is carried out by the aircraft commander designated by the aircraft operator according to the standards, conditions and procedures specified in the training program and operational guidance documents;
e) For single-pilot operations, en-route operations checks are performed in an environment representative of the actual operating conditions of single-pilot operations;
g) Contents of in-flight operations inspection on aircraft periodically every 12 months are specified in Appendix LII attached to these Regulations."},"880":{"id":"880","code":"Điều 880","title":"Điều 880. Huấn luyện định kỳ đối với cơ giới trên không và dẫn đường trên không","chapter":"Chương III - HUẤN LUYỆN VÀ KIỂM TRA ĐỊNH KỲ CỦA NGƯỜI KHAI THÁC TÀU BAY","partTitle":"Phần XIV - YÊU CẦU VỀ TRÌNH ĐỘ ĐỐI VỚI NHÂN VIÊN HÀNG KHÔNG CỦA NGƯỜI KHAI THÁC CÓ GIẤY CHỨNG NHẬN NGƯỜI KHAI THÁC TÀU BAY","partId":"main_part_14","plainText":"Điều 880. Huấn luyện định kỳ đối với cơ giới trên không và dẫn đường trên không
