c) Training on a flight simulator or on an aircraft includes the following contents:
Section
Training content
Manage normal, abnormal and emergency situations
High-risk content is conducted on appropriate flight simulators when available and available, including management of fire, smoke, and toxic vapor situations in flight; approach and landing under abnormal flight control conditions; loss of tail rotor efficiency; recovery from abnormal flight state; Operate in conditions of impaired or loss of vision due to brownout, whiteout effects when applied; Emergency procedures have a high level of risk and their implementation on an aircraft can directly affect flight safety. Content that can be performed on aircraft or flight training devices includes operations in adverse weather conditions; unusual and emergency procedures of helicopters; diversion flights and takeoff and landing sites or alternate airports; engine failure and single engine operation for multi-engine helicopters; emergency landing, autorotation when applicable and does not include the mandatory requirement to perform a complete autorotation on the aircraft; in-flight fuel management; re-flight procedures.
Flight training based on actual operational scenarios
Carry out flights according to the actual operating environment of the aircraft operator; Practice emergency and complex situations on flight lines, rigs or restricted areas when applicable; Flight crew coordination procedures and coordination with air traffic service providers.
Capacity assessment
The test script is not announced in advance to ensure assessment effectiveness; The final test is performed on the aircraft or flight training simulator, integrated with the type rating test when applicable and performed by a flight examiner from the Civil Aviation Authority of Vietnam.
Operation in low visibility conditions and zero-flight training
Procedures for takeoff and landing in limited visibility conditions; circling and visual approach on a flight simulator.
Preventing and recovering abnormal aircraft positions
Autopilot system management, including failure cases; recognize and recover from abnormal flight conditions.
The number of training sessions on simulators or on aircraft is determined by the aircraft operator in the training and testing program but must not be less than 05 training sessions, excluding training sessions for special forms of operations, air-time training and competency assessment tests.
d) After meeting the requirements of the training phase on a flight simulator or on an aircraft, the pilot shall perform flight operations under supervision and inspection of operations on the flight path according to the following requirements:
Phase
Request
Fly operations under supervision
The number of flight segments is determined by the aircraft operator in the training program but must not be lower than 20 flights or 40 flights under supervision; performed in the designated seat position for the main pilot under the supervision of a flight examiner or flight instructor; Perform all duties and responsibilities of the main pilot during flights under supervision.
Checking flight operations at the end of the program
Conducted by flight examiners of the Vietnam Aviation Administration.
5. The main pilot upgrade training program combined with aircraft type conversion applies to co-pilots upgraded to main pilots on aircraft types other than those currently in operation and includes the following contents:
a) Aircraft conversion training program;
b) Main pilot upgrade training program and inspection as prescribed in Clause 3 or Clause 4 of this Article appropriate to the type of aircraft;
c) Additional training on different types of operations, procedures and policies of aircraft operators for new aircraft types.
6. The pilot upgrade training program must be integrated into the aircraft operator's training program before implementation.
7. Standards for completing the main driver upgrade training program:
a) Pass the test conducted by a flight examiner or flight supervisor according to the provisions of Clause 3 or Clause 4 of this Article and meet the standards prescribed by the aircraft operator on the basis of the aircraft pilot's competency framework, including KNO, PRO, COM, FPA, FPM, LTW, PSD, SAW, WLM or based on the content and assessment criteria in the aircraft operator's training program;
b) The aircraft operator stipulates in the training program the maximum number of retests and the content and duration of additional training to complete the main pilot upgrade training program."},"translatedText":"Article 909. Main driver upgrade training program

