Article 910. Airport operations qualifications for aircraft pilots
1. Airport familiarization training includes knowledge about obstacles, airport floor plans, lighting systems, access aids, arrival methods, departure methods, holding flight methods, instrument approach methods, applicable minimum operating standards and notes when aircraft move on the ground.
2. Aircraft operators classify airports in the operating manual and fully inform flight crew members and flight dispatchers of the list of airports classified as A, B or C.
3. All airports to which aircraft operators operate are classified according to one of the following three types:
Airport type
Classification criteria
Type A
The airport has a straight 3D instrument approach with a glideslope angle not exceeding 3.5 degrees for each runway intended to be used for landing; There is at least one runway that does not have a procedure that is limited by performance for takeoff, landing or critical engine failure during the takeoff phase and is capable of night operations.
Type B
The airport does not fully meet the criteria for a Class A airport or has unique characteristics, including approach aids or non-standard approach trails; unusual weather conditions, environmental features that may cause turbulence, wind shear or unusual wind conditions; unusual characteristics or limitations of features; Unusual characteristics of the runway in terms of length, width, slope, signal markings or lighting system create an unusual visual angle when approaching; Obstacles, floor plans, lighting systems, restrictions on circling in certain areas due to obstacles in the circling area or requirements for training and experience of flight crew members published in aeronautical information bulletins or identified in operations manuals but do not include requirements for training on a flight simulator or aerodrome familiarization flights.
Type C
Airports with specific characteristics requiring additional operational requirements beyond those applicable to Class B airports or with requirements for experience and qualifications of flight crew members published in the aeronautical information bulletin or identified in the operational manual, including training on simulators or airport familiarization flights.
4. Offshore takeoff and landing grounds are classified by aircraft operators as type B or type C airports based on assessment of the complexity of operations, environmental conditions, methods of approach, takeoff, landing, obstacles, equipment, communications, navigation and emergency support capacity, specifically as follows:
a) Offshore take-off and landing sites are classified as type B airports when they have different operating characteristics compared to conventional airports and landing sites, but safety risks are controlled by self-study of documents, explanations, familiarity with operating methods and compliance with the operating procedures prescribed by the aircraft operator;
b) An offshore take-off and landing site is classified as a type C airport when it has complex operational characteristics, limitations on access, take-off, landing areas, obstacles, meteorological conditions, sea surface state, movement of offshore structures, equipment, communications, navigation or emergency support capabilities, requiring aircraft pilots to complete training, testing or familiarization flights according to the content prescribed by the aircraft operator before operating.
5. Before operating to a Class B airport planned as an arrival airport or a mandatory alternate airport, the pilot shall complete consultation or self-study on additional specific characteristics applicable to operations to that Class B airport. Completion of consultation or self-study is recorded and confirmed upon completion or confirmed by the aircraft commander prior to departure of a flight with a Class B airport as the arrival airport or mandatory alternate airport.
6. Before operating to a Class C airport planned as an arrival airport or a mandatory alternate airport, the pilot must meet the following requirements:
a) Comply with the operating requirements applicable to that airport as published in the aeronautical information bulletin or specified in the aircraft operator's operating instructions;
b) Complete consultation or self-study on additional specific features applicable to operations to that Class C airport;
c) Conduct a practical familiarization flight to that aerodrome as an observer or complete training on an appropriate flight simulator;
d) In case of a familiarization flight as an observer, the pilot sits in the observation seat when the aircraft is equipped with an observation seat; In case there is no observation seat or it is not possible to install an observation seat, the aircraft pilot may sit in the pilot's seat to familiarize himself with the airport with the aircraft commander who has appropriate qualifications to operate type C airports and is designated by the aircraft operator.
7. For helicopters, the aircraft pilot conducts 01 familiarization flight to the airport, helipad or take-off and landing site with characteristics similar to the operations that the aircraft pilot is assigned to perform. The familiarization flight includes at least 01 landing at an airport, heliport or representative take-off and landing site, in the role of a flight crew member and under the supervision of an aircraft pilot who is appropriately qualified for that operation.
8. Requirements for airport operations, except for requirements for heliports, helicopter take-off and landing sites, do not apply when operations meet the following requirements at the same time:
a) Done during daylight hours;
b) Actual visibility is at least 03 Nm or 5,000 m;
c) The cloud ceiling at an aerodrome is at least 1,000 ft above the lowest initial approach altitude for an instrument approach procedure at that aerodrome."},"translatedText":"Article 910. Airport operations qualifications for aircraft pilots

