3. In case the flight attendant needs more time than the flight crew to carry out preparation and pre-flight consultation for the same flight or a series of flights, the aircraft operator is allowed to adjust the flight duty time as follows:
a) The flight crew's flight duty time is allowed to be extended by a period of time corresponding to the difference in time of arrival for duty between the flight attendant and the flight crew, the difference must not exceed 01 hour;
b) The flight crew's maximum daily flight duty time limit is compared and determined based on the time the flight crew is present to take on duty;
c) The flight attendant's actual flight duty time is calculated from the time the flight attendant arrives to receive the assignment.
4. The maximum daily FDP of flight crew members who do not rest during the flight and have synchronized circadian rhythms is extended as follows:
a) The maximum flight duty time per day is only allowed to be extended by a maximum of 01 hour and the frequency must not exceed 02 times in 07 consecutive days, and the aircraft operator must meet the following regulations on compensatory rest time: Increase 02 hours for both pre-flight rest time and post-flight rest time or add 04 hours for post-flight rest time;
b) In case the aircraft operator extends the flight duty time for two consecutive cycles, the rest time before and after these two extensions must be added to the corresponding compensatory rest period specified in Point a of this Clause;
c) The extension of flight duty time must be planned in advance and comply with the specific number of flight segments as follows: maximum 05 flight segments in case of not violating the low circadian rhythm time frame; Maximum 04 flight sectors in case of violation of low circadian rhythm time frame of 02 hours or less; Maximum 02 flight sectors in case the low circadian rhythm time frame is violated by more than 02 hours;
d) Do not combine the extension of flight duty time specified in this Article with in-flight rest time or interrupted flight schedules within the same duty cycle;
d) The fatigue management program must clearly stipulate the limit of maximum daily FDP extension according to the type of operation based on the number of flight segments and whether or not FDP is within the low circadian rhythm hours.
5. Where an extension of the maximum daily FDP due to in-flight rest periods is applicable, the fatigue management program must clearly define the applicable requirements for the extension of the maximum daily flight duty time, in accordance with the certification standards applicable to each type of operation, taking into account the following factors:
a) Number of flight sectors;
b) Minimum in-flight rest time for each flight crew member;
c) Type of in-flight rest facilities;
d) Increased flight crew composition."},"translatedText":"Article 922. Flight duty time

