Article 987. Weight limits by performance for general aviation
1. Aircraft operators must ensure that the weight of the aircraft at the time of starting the takeoff run does not exceed the maximum takeoff weight specified in the flight manual in accordance with the actual barometric altitude and meteorological conditions, and at the same time ensure the following capabilities:
a) For multi-engine aircraft: If a critical engine fails at any time after reaching the decision speed, the aircraft must be able to continue takeoff and reach a safe altitude to overcome obstacles;
b) For all aircraft: If a failure occurs before reaching the decision speed, the aircraft must be able to stop safely within the available runway length.
2. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that the weight of the aircraft allows maintaining the minimum climb speed and the ability to overcome obstacles along the flight path in case of critical engine failure for multi-engine aircraft.
3. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that the weight of the aircraft at the time of expected landing does not exceed the following limits:
a) Maximum landing weight specified in the Flight Instructions is appropriate to the barometric altitude and meteorological conditions;
b) Maximum weight according to Noise Certificate.
4. When determining the limits in this Article, aircraft operators take into account:
a) Expected weight loss due to fuel consumption in flight;
b) Fuel discharge to achieve safe landing weight according to procedures specified in the Operations Manual."},"translatedText":"Article 987. Weight limits by performance for general aviation

