Article 991. Data and documents for calculating weight and balance
1. Aircraft operators must establish weight and balance data, and document aircraft weight and balance calculations before each flight, in which the aircraft weight and balance calculation documents must allow the aircraft commander to determine that the load and load distribution are guaranteed not to exceed the aircraft weight and balance limits.
2. Weight and balance calculation documents must contain the following content:
a) Type and registration of aircraft;
b) Name, flight number and date;
c) Name of the aircraft commander;
d) Name of person preparing documents;
d) Empty operational weight and corresponding center of gravity of the aircraft;
e) Fuel weight at take-off and fuel for the flight;
g) Weight of consumed liquids other than fuel;
h) Load composition includes passengers, luggage, cargo and aircraft's center of gravity adjustment loads;
i) Takeoff weight, landing weight and empty weight;
k) Position of aircraft center of gravity;
l) Weight limits and center of gravity values.
3. In case data and documents calculating aircraft weight and balance are created using a computer system, the aircraft operator must:
a) Periodically check the integrity of the output data to ensure that the data is within the limits specified in the Flight Manual by checking that modifications to the input data are accurately updated into the system and that the system is maintained to operate correctly on an ongoing basis;
b) Specify procedures for using that computer system in the aircraft operator's operating manual.
4. The aircraft loading supervisor must confirm by direct signature or electronic signature that the load and load distribution are consistent with the weight and balance calculation documents provided to the aircraft commander, and the aircraft commander must confirm approval by direct signature or electronic signature method.
5. Aircraft operators must establish procedures for handling last-minute load changes to ensure that:
a) Any last-minute changes after completion of the weight and balance calculation documents are notified to the aircraft commander and recorded in the flight preparation documents containing the weight and balance documents;
b) Determine the maximum allowable limit for last-minute changes in passenger numbers or cargo load and re-document new weight and balance calculations if this maximum limit is exceeded.
6. Where weight and balance calculation documents are transmitted to the aircraft via electronic data transmission, a copy of the final aircraft weight and balance documents approved by the aircraft commander must be kept on the ground.
7. General aviation operators using non-complex power aircraft are not required to apply the provisions of this Article but are responsible for ensuring compliance with weight limits and load distribution specified in the aircraft's flight manual and operations manual."},"translatedText":"Article 991. Data and documents for calculating weight and balance

