Article 1047. Requirements for RNAV 1 and RNAV 2 exploitation
1. RNAV 1 or RNAV 2 operations are conducted in airspace, air traffic routes, standard instrument departure procedures, standard instrument arrival procedures or stages of published instrument approach procedures applying RNAV 1 or RNAV 2.
2. RNAV 1 and RNAV 2 are applicable until the final approach landmark or final approach point when declared applicable.
3. The navigation system used for RNAV 1 or RNAV 2 uses navigation signal sources from the global navigation satellite system, DME/DME, DME/DME combined with inertial navigation system or other navigation signal source when appropriately identified in the flight manual or technical documents issued by the organization responsible for designing the aircraft type.
4. In case RNAV 1 or RNAV 2 relies on DME/DME or DME/DME combined inertial navigation system, flight planning must consider DME infrastructure, DME equipment status, aeronautical information announcements, areas where DME coverage is not guaranteed and related contingency plans.
5. Routes, standard instrument departure procedures, standard instrument arrival procedures or phases of instrument approach procedures applying RNAV 1 or RNAV 2 must be loaded from a valid navigation database, except in cases where the construction of routes using route points from the published navigation database and is permitted by the operations manual.
6. Before implementing the RNAV 1 or RNAV 2 procedure, the flight crew member is responsible for checking the procedure name, takeoff and landing routes, transition points, sequence of flight points, transition types between flight segments, altitude limits, speed limits and flight paths displayed on the aircraft system.
7. In case the air traffic service provider issues a clearance to fly according to a vector, fly straight to a specified point, fly off the route or change the procedure, the flight crew member is responsible for ensuring that the modification of the flight path in the aircraft system does not distort the flight path or procedure and must continue to be implemented.
8. After receiving a clearance to fly along a vector or to deviate from the loaded flight path, a flight crew member is not allowed to modify the currently operating flight path in the aircraft system in a way that eliminates the ability to return to the flight path or procedure, except in the case of receiving a new clearance or re-confirming the flight path, which must be done with the air traffic service provider.
9. Abnormal procedures and contingency procedures for RNAV 1 or RNAV 2 shall include addressing the loss or deterioration of navigation signal sources, excessive flight path deviations, inadequate navigation databases, failure of essential ground navigation equipment, inadequate air traffic service facility clearances, or loss of ability to respond to RNAV 1 or RNAV 2."},"translatedText":"Article 1047. Requirements for RNAV 1 and RNAV 2 exploitation

