Article 1056. Requirements for exploitation safety assessment
1. Aircraft operators must perform an operational safety assessment before performing RNP AR APCH, RNP AR DP or PBN operations that require stricter control of the aircraft, systems, procedures, crew members, operating conditions or contingency plans.
2. Operational safety assessment must be consistent with aircraft type, aircraft configuration, navigation system, navigation standards, RNP value, flight route, airspace, procedure, airport, runway and expected operating conditions.
3. Operational safety assessment must consider the aircraft's capacity, maintenance program, minimum equipment list, navigation database, external operating conditions, operational procedures, training program, inspection program, capacity of flight crew members, flight dispatchers, maintenance personnel and backup plans.
4. Operational safety assessment for RNP AR APCH must consider initial approach, mid approach, final approach, reentry, RNP value, vertical flight data, RF flight function, terrain, obstacles, runway, meteorological conditions, prevailing barometric pressure at the airport and approach abort criteria.
5. Operational safety assessment for RNP AR DP must consider take-off performance, altitude gain feature, departure climb slope, post-takeoff turning point, speed limit, terrain, obstacles, runway conditions, meteorological conditions, engine failure and post-takeoff contingency plan.
6. The mining safety assessment must identify risks, mitigation measures, exploitation limits, applicable conditions, implementation responsibilities and content that needs to be included in the exploitation manual, training program, inspection program, maintenance program, minimum equipment list and exploitation monitoring process.
7. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that mitigation measures identified in the operational safety assessment are implemented before carrying out the respective operational activity.
8. Results of operational safety assessment, mitigation measures and related data must be kept, updated and ready for the Civil Aviation Authority of Vietnam to check when necessary.
9. Aircraft operators are responsible for reviewing and updating operational safety assessments when there are changes affecting the aircraft, navigation system, software, navigation database, navigation standards, RNP values, routes, airspace, procedures, airports, runways, navigation infrastructure, air traffic services, operating conditions, training programs, maintenance programs or minimum equipment list.
10. Aircraft operators are not allowed to continue performing PBN operations when the operational safety assessment shows that risks have not been controlled or mitigation measures have not been fully implemented."},"translatedText":"Article 1056. Requirements for exploitation safety assessment

