Article 1057. Requirements for monitoring, reporting and analyzing PBN exploitation data
1. Aircraft operators are responsible for developing, promulgating, implementing and maintaining procedures for collecting, recording, analyzing and handling events related to PBN operations.
2. Flight crew members are responsible for reporting to the aircraft operator when one of the following events occurs:
a) The aircraft is no longer capable of meeting declared or currently applicable navigation standards;
b) Raising an RNP failure warning or navigation feature warning;
c) Horizontal or vertical flight path deviation exceeds the allowable limit;
d) Loss, deterioration or failure to ensure the integrity of the navigation signal source;
d) Failure of the navigation system, flight management system, display system, flight guidance system, autopilot system or related warning system;
e) The navigation database is invalid, missing, distorted or inconsistent with the aeronautical information bulletin set, aeronautical information notice or related procedure chart;
g) Unable to execute the clearance of the air traffic service provider due to limitations in the aircraft's navigation capacity or characteristics of the procedure;
h) Must cancel the procedure, re-fly, fly off the route or switch to a backup plan due to reasons related to PBN operations;
i) Terrain warnings, obstacle warnings or other unusual events arise during the execution of RNP APCH, RNP AR APCH, RNP AR DP, RNP 0.3 for helicopters or A-RNP.
3. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that flight dispatchers, maintenance personnel and related personnel report events or changes that may affect the aircraft's ability to meet navigation standards or the conditions for performing PBN operations.
4. Event analysis related to PBN operations must consider navigation standards, RNP values, routes, airspace, procedures, airports, operational conditions, navigation system status, navigation signal sources, navigation databases, operational procedures, training, meteorological conditions, terrain, obstacles, air traffic services and mitigation measures identified in the operational safety assessment when applicable.
5. For RNP AR APCH, the aircraft operator is responsible for monitoring and analyzing the number of procedure executions, number of approach or reentry aborts, RNP failure warnings, off-limit flight path deviations, terrain warnings, navigation database errors, vertical flight data errors, RF flight function data errors, causes of each event and corresponding corrective measures.
6. For RNP AR DP, the aircraft operator is responsible for monitoring and analyzing the number of procedure executions, the number of procedure aborts or switching to backup plans, RNP non-response warnings, off-limit flight path deviations, terrain warnings, events related to takeoff features, altitude gain features, departure climb slope, ability to overcome obstacles, navigation database errors, RF flight function data errors, causes of each event and its respective remedy.
7. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that events related to PBN operations are classified according to the degree of impact on operational safety and are handled in the aircraft operator's safety management system.
8. Aircraft operators are responsible for determining, implementing, monitoring and evaluating the effectiveness of corrective measures for events related to PBN operations.
9. Monitoring results, event reports, cause analysis, corrective measures and effectiveness assessment results must be kept and ready for the Vietnam Aviation Administration to check when necessary.
10. Aircraft operators are responsible for notifying the Vietnam Aviation Administration of serious events related to PBN operations according to regulations on mandatory aviation safety reporting.
11. Aircraft operators are not allowed to continue performing PBN operations when the results of monitoring or data analysis show that the aircraft, systems, navigation database, flight crew members, operating procedures, maintenance programs or mitigation measures no longer meet applicable requirements."},"translatedText":"Article 1057. Requirements for monitoring, reporting and analyzing PBN exploitation data

