Article 1073. Takeoff in poor visibility conditions for helicopters
1. For takeoff operations in limited visibility conditions of helicopters with a runway visibility value of less than 400 m at a land airport or a takeoff and landing site with equipment departure, the aircraft operator is responsible for ensuring the runway visibility value or visibility according to support equipment at the land-based operating facility is determined as follows:
Runway visibility or visibility
Equipment
Not less than 250 m or take-off hold distance, whichever is greater
No lights and no signal markings, only applicable during the day
Not less than 800 m
No signal paint, applied at night
Not less than 200 m
Lighting of runway curbs or final approach and takeoff areas, and road centerline markings
Not less than 150 m
Runway or final approach and takeoff area lighting, runway centerline markings and related runway visibility information
2. For take-off operations from offshore take-off and landing sites with an obstacle-free take-off trajectory, aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that the runway visibility value is not less than the following values:
a) 500 m for single-pilot operations;
b) 250 m for multi-member crew operations."},"translatedText":"Article 1073. Takeoff in poor visibility conditions for helicopters

