Article 1077. Access with Class I equipment applies exploitation credits
1. For approach operations using type I equipment applying operation credits, the aircraft operator is responsible for ensuring that the decision altitude is not lower than the largest of the following values:
a) The minimum decision altitude value is specified in the aircraft's flight manual;
b) Obstacle clearance altitude applicable to the corresponding aircraft class;
c) The decided altitude value is appropriate to the scope of training, testing and capabilities of flight crew members;
d) 150 ft.
2. In case the decision altitude is lower than 200 ft, the aircraft operator is responsible for ensuring this value is determined by radio altimeter.
3. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that approach operations using Class I equipment applying operation credits are performed when the following visual aids are ready for operation:
a) Approach light system as prescribed in Table 8;
b) Paint the runway with precise approach markings;
c) Type I runway lighting system.
4. The minimum runway visibility value is not lower than the largest of the following values:
a) The minimum runway visibility value is specified in the aircraft's flight manual, if any;
b) Minimum runway visibility value specified in Clause 5 of this Article.
5. The minimum runway visibility value for approach operations using type I equipment applying operation credits, according to the decision altitude and approach lighting system, is determined as follows:
