Article 1083. Mining procedures for access mining activities using type II equipment
1. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that Class II instrument approach operations are carried out with a flight crew consisting of at least two flight crew members.
2. The aircraft operator is responsible for ensuring that the approach method is performed using the system specified in the aircraft's flight manual or documents issued by the organization responsible for designing the aircraft type.
3. Where the approach is performed using an autopilot and a manual landing, the aircraft operator is responsible for ensuring the autopilot remains connected until the necessary visual reference is obtained by the flight crew member.
4. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that altitude commands are below 200 ft above the runway elevation determined by radio altimeter.
5. The aircraft operator is responsible for ensuring that the decision altitude is determined by a radio altimeter when required by the aircraft flight manual or documents issued by the organization responsible for designing the aircraft type.
6. At decision altitude, the aircraft operator is responsible for ensuring that the following visual references are clearly displayed and recognized by flight crew members:
a) The segment includes at least three consecutive lights, which are center lights of the approach light system, touchdown zone lights, runway center lights, take-off and landing curb lights, or a combination of these lights;
b) Visual reference includes at least one horizontal element of the ground signaling system such as the horizontal light strip of the approach lighting system, runway apron or touchdown zone light strip, except where operations are carried out using a system that displays information on the windshield up to the touchdown point."},"translatedText":"Article 1083. Mining procedures for access mining activities using type II equipment

