Article 1107. Planning standards for EDTO reserve airport
1. An airport is selected as an EDTO reserve airport when the meteorological bulletin, meteorological forecast or a combination of current meteorological information shows that meteorological conditions at that airport are expected to remain equal to or higher than the applicable planning standards during the period from the earliest expected time of landing to the latest expected time of landing at the EDTO reserve airport.
2. Minimum operational standards for planning for EDTO reserve airport are determined as follows:
Airport approach
Cloud ceiling height
The aerodrome has at least one operating navigation facility for a non-precision straight approach or a CAT I precision approach or a circling approach from an instrument approach.
Add 400 ft to MDA(H) or DA(H)
The airport has at least two operating navigation facilities, each providing a direct approach to a different suitable runway.
Add 200 ft to the higher DA(H) or MDA(H) of the two approaches used.
An ILS CAT II approach is permitted.
300 ft
An ILS CAT III approach is permitted.
200 ft
3. The aircraft operator is responsible for specifying in the operating manual the method for determining planning standards for the EDTO reserve airport, the applicable time period for the expected time of landing and the responsibility for assessing meteorological conditions before the flight."},"translatedText":"Article 1107. Planning standards for EDTO reserve airport

