Article 1114. EDTO Maintenance and Reliability Program
1. The aircraft operator is responsible for ensuring that the EDTO maintenance program and EDTO reliability program are established, maintained and applied in accordance with the aircraft and engine combination, configuration documents, maintenance and EDTO procedures and expected EDTO operations.
2. EDTO maintenance program includes the following contents:
a) EDTO maintenance work for engines, auxiliary power equipment, critical systems for EDTO operations and critical systems for EDTO operations has a time limit;
b) Procedures for controlling duplicate maintenance work for the same critical system operating EDTO to reduce the risk of common errors or repeat errors after maintenance;
c) Procedures for controlling spare parts, supplies, replacement equipment and technical configuration applied to key EDTO exploitation systems;
d) Procedures for checking the technical condition of the aircraft, engine and key systems operating EDTO before putting the aircraft on an EDTO flight;
d) Procedures for evaluating and handling failures, technical defects or applicable content in the list of minimum equipment that affects EDTO exploitation.
3. EDTO reliability program includes the following contents:
a) Monitoring of in-flight engine shutdowns and technical events related to the ability to continue EDTO flight;
b) Monitor engine condition, engine performance trends and related technical parameters;
c) Monitor oil consumption of the engine and auxiliary power supply equipment;
d) Monitor the ability to start the auxiliary power supply device during flight, when the auxiliary power supply device is a key system for EDTO operations or has an impact on EDTO operations;
d) Monitor failures, performance degradation or technical events related to critical EDTO exploitation systems and time-limited EDTO exploitation critical systems.
4. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that data collected from the EDTO reliability program is stored, evaluated and analyzed to identify adverse trends, technical causes, corrective measures and effects on maximum diversion times or EDTO operations.
5. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that incidents, failures or technical events affecting EDTO operations are reported, evaluated and handled according to the EDTO maintenance program, EDTO reliability program and the aircraft operator's safety management system.
6. The aircraft operator is responsible for specifying in the maintenance documents or operating manuals the EDTO maintenance control method, the EDTO reliability program, reporting responsibilities, data analysis and corrective measures for technical events related to EDTO."},"translatedText":"Article 1114. EDTO Maintenance and Reliability Program

