Article 1126. Training and testing for electronic flight documentation application
1. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that flight crew members, flight operations dispatchers, operations control personnel, electronic flight documentation management personnel, technical personnel and other relevant personnel complete appropriate training before using, administering or supporting the application of Class B electronic flight documents or electronic flight documentation functions that directly serve safe operations.
2. Training for flight crew members includes the following contents:
a) Concept, structure, equipment configuration, applications, functions, limitations of use and flight periods when electronic flight documents can be used;
b) Check device status, power supply, battery, connection, data, software version and database before flight;
c) Normal, abnormal, contingency procedures and operating conditions when electronic flight documents do not function normally;
d) Cross-check, detect major errors and handle discrepancies in aircraft performance data, weight and load distribution, airport data, flight route data, meteorology, aviation news announcements and other operational information;
d) Handling cases of inconsistent information between electronic flight documentation devices, between electronic flight documentation and the cockpit system or between electronic flight documentation and other information sources;
e) Handling cases of single device failure, multiple device failure, application loss, data loss, connection loss, power loss, overheating, electromagnetic interference or security incidents;
g) Human factors, human-machine interaction interface, workload, task assignment, coordination, cross-supervision and flight crew resource management;
h) Report errors, failures, incorrect data, erroneous information, security incidents or situations that increase safety risks related to electronic flight documents.
3. Training for flight dispatchers and operations control personnel includes content appropriate to the responsibilities of preparing flights, issuing flight plans, providing data, updating operational information, supporting flight crew members and handling cases where electronic flight documents or related data do not function normally.
4. Training for electronic flight document management personnel and technical personnel includes system configuration, hardware, software, database management, access rights, security, backup, restore, update, maintenance, error handling, change management and operations support.
5. Aircraft operators are responsible for conducting periodic training, differentiated training or updated training when there are changes to equipment, applications, functions, operating systems, databases, operating procedures, backup plans, evaluated usage content or limits on the use of electronic flight documents.
6. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that the inspection of electronic flight documents is consistent with the functions and tasks of personnel specified in Clause 1 of this Article.
7. Aircraft operators are responsible for storing training and checking results for electronic flight documents according to applicable regulations on retention of training documents."},"translatedText":"Article 1126. Training and testing for electronic flight documentation application

