Chương VIIIKHAI THÁC MÁY BAY MỘT ĐỘNG CƠ TUABIN BAN ĐÊM HOẶC TRONG IMC TRONG VẬN TẢI HÀNG KHÔNG THƯƠNG MẠI</p>","translations":{"en":"Article 1128. Change management, reporting and data retention for electronic flight documentation applications

Article 1128. Change management, reporting and data retention for electronic flight documentation applications
1. Aircraft operators are responsible for implementing change management before putting into use in flight operations changes to equipment, operating systems, applications, functions, software versions, databases, data sources, connections, operating procedures, training programs, backup plans or content using electronic flight documents that have been evaluated.
2. The following changes must be safety assessed and relevant documents updated before being used in flight operations:
a) Putting into use new electronic flight documentation equipment, new hardware platforms or new arrangements in the cockpit;
b) Putting into use a new Type B electronic flight documentation application or a new function of a Type B electronic flight documentation application;
c) Change the calculation algorithm, data processing method, input data, output results or usage limits of the Type B electronic flight documentation application;
d) Changing the human-machine interaction interface of the Type B electronic flight documentation application, causing changes in operational procedures or training programs;
d) Expand the use of electronic flight documents to other aircraft types, aircraft variants, aircraft fleets, types of operations or flight phases;
e) Changing the operational plan from having backup paper documents to paperless operations or changing operational conditions when electronic flight documents do not function normally;
g) Change the connection to the aircraft system, aircraft power source, communication device with the aircraft system or data transmission and reception mechanism for operations.
3. The following changes are made according to the aircraft operator's internal control procedures if they do not change the evaluated usage content, calculation algorithms, functions, human-machine interaction interface, operating procedures, training programs or operating conditions when electronic flight documents do not function normally:
a) Operating system updates, patches or supporting software do not affect the Class B electronic flight documentation application;
b) Update databases periodically, including maps, airports, flight routes, meteorology or other operational data;
c) Bug fixes, security updates, or configuration adjustments do not affect the functionality, output, or flow of use of the Class B electronic flight documentation application.
4. Aircraft operators are responsible for testing, inspecting, recording configurations and retaining documents proving the changes specified in Clauses 2 and 3 of this Article before being used in flight operations.
5. Aircraft operators are responsible for establishing reporting, analysis and handling procedures for errors, failures, data corruption, overheating, electromagnetic interference, security incidents, misuse or situations that increase safety risks related to electronic flight documents.
6. Aircraft operators are responsible for maintaining documents proving compliance with requirements, safety risk assessment, results of operational assessment, system configuration, software version, database version, changes, training, error reports and corrective measures related to electronic flight documents.
7. In case a change, error, corruption or data corruption is detected that causes safety risks to exceed an acceptable level, the aircraft operator is responsible for applying appropriate risk control measures and suspending use of the application or related function until the safety risk is controlled at an acceptable level.
