Article 1130. Reliability of turbine engines
1. Aircraft operators are responsible for collecting and maintaining data on the reliability of the aircraft-engine combination from the organization responsible for designing the aircraft type, the organization responsible for designing design changes, or the appropriate source of operational data.
2. Data on the aircraft-engine combination must have a basis showing that the rate of loss of power is less than ten cases per million flight hours.
3. Loss of power includes any reduction in power, including an engine stall in flight, that results from a design or installation defect in the engine or engine component, including the auxiliary fuel system or engine control system.
4. The operating experience of the aircraft-engine combination must reach a minimum of 100,000 flight hours to demonstrate the required level of reliability.
5. In case the number of flight hours specified in Clause 4 of this Article has not been accumulated, documents proving compliance with the requirements must include results of technical analysis, test results or operational experience of similar or related aircraft-engine combinations to determine the ability to meet the requirements of design, system features and reliability for the operation of single-engine turbine aircraft at night or in instrument flight meteorological conditions.
6. Aircraft operators are responsible for monitoring engine trends, evaluating incident reports, safety threats, and updating the reliability program when there are signs of deterioration in the reliability of the aircraft-engine combination."},"translatedText":"Article 1130. Reliability of turbine engines

