Article 1138. Operational guidance documents, operational limits and flight planning
1. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that helicopters used in class 3 performance operations in instrument meteorological conditions are certified to be airworthy for operation according to instrument flight rules.
2. Aircraft operators are responsible for stipulating in the operating manual the following contents for the operation of performance class 3 helicopters in instrument meteorological conditions:
a) Limits and conditions of exploitation;
b) Normal, abnormal and emergency mining procedures;
c) Area or route of operation;
d) Intended landing location and appropriate forced landing location;
d) Minimum exploitation standards;
e) Flight crew composition, task assignment and conditions for using the autopilot system, if applicable;
g) Other necessary information related to mining activities.
3. The aircraft operator is responsible for specifying in the operating manual the flight planning criteria to consider the possibility of safely performing a forced landing in the event of engine failure or major damage.
4. Flight planning criteria include the following elements:
a) Terrain flown over;
b) Meteorological conditions, including seasonal meteorological influences and other meteorological conditions that may affect the flight;
c) Suitable landing location;
d) Operational limits of helicopters;
d) Other criteria and limitations apply to the area or flight route operated.
5. In case the autopilot system is used in the flight crew composition plan or to reduce the pilot's workload, the aircraft operator is responsible for stipulating the conditions, limits of use and backup plans when the autopilot system fails or does not work in the operating manual.
6. Aircraft operators are responsible for specifying in the list of minimum equipment the minimum operational requirements for systems and equipment used for operating performance class 3 helicopters in instrument flight meteorological conditions."},"translatedText":"Article 1138. Operational guidance documents, operational limits and flight planning

