Article 1147. Use of offshore take-off and landing sites
1. Aircraft operators can only use offshore takeoff and landing sites when ensuring that the takeoff and landing sites are suitable for the type of helicopter being operated, expected operating conditions and applicable operating limits.
2. Aircraft operators are responsible for establishing, maintaining and updating the list of offshore takeoff and landing sites expected to be used in the operating manual. This list includes at least the following information:
a) Physical characteristics, size, load-bearing capacity and operating limits of the take-off and landing site;
b) Obstacles, obstacle protection areas, restrictions or warnings that affect mining activities;
c) Conditions of visual aids, lighting systems, meteorological information, communication and navigation;
d) Conditions for rescue, firefighting, refueling and exploitation support equipment;
d) Applicable limits, restrictions or conditions of exploitation, including cases of daytime exploitation only, performance limitations or environmental conditions limitations.
3. Before putting an offshore takeoff and landing site into operation, the aircraft operator is responsible for assessing the suitability of that takeoff and landing site based on at least the following contents:
a) Physical characteristics and compatibility with operational helicopters;
b) Ensure the maintenance of obstacle protection areas for approach and take-off activities;
c) Conditions of the yard surface, lighting system, signal paint and equipment to support exploitation;
d) Environmental conditions that affect operational safety, including aerodynamic turbulence, exhaust gas, heat sources, and foreign objects;
d) Ability for rescue, firefighting, communication, meteorology and personnel serving takeoff and landing sites.
4. In case information about offshore take-off and landing sites is incomplete, aircraft operators may only operate on the basis of safety assessment and apply appropriate operational restrictions until completing the assessment and confirming the suitability of that take-off and landing site.
5. In cases where an offshore takeoff or landing site does not fully meet the applicable requirements, the aircraft operator may use that takeoff or landing site when the safety risk assessment determines that the proposed operations can be carried out safely with additional operational restrictions and control measures."},"translatedText":"Article 1147. Use of offshore take-off and landing sites

