Article 1148. Selection of airports and reserve take-off and landing sites
1. Aircraft operators are not required to designate an alternate landing airport in the operational flight plan for a flight from an offshore takeoff or landing site to an onshore landing airport when there is an appropriate operational backup plan to ensure the ability to safely return from the sea.
2. Aircraft operators can only choose reserve offshore takeoff and landing sites when they meet the following requirements:
a) Reserve offshore take-off and landing sites may only be used after the point of no return, except in cases where there is no geographically suitable reserve land-landing site on land. Before the point of no return, a reserve airport must be used to land on the mainland;
b) The helicopter is capable of landing at a reserve offshore take-off and landing site in case one engine fails to operate;
c) To the extent possible, the availability of reserve offshore take-off and landing sites is confirmed before the point of no return. The size, configuration and obstacle clearance of the take-off and landing site or intended use location is appropriate to the type of helicopter being operated;
d) Minimum weather standards are established taking into account the accuracy and reliability of meteorological information;
d) List of minimum equipment with specific regulations applicable to this type of exploitation;
e) Aircraft operators develop procedures for selecting reserve offshore takeoff and landing sites in the operating manual."},"translatedText":"Article 1148. Selection of airports and reserve take-off and landing sites

