Article 1157. Additional procedures and equipment for mining operations in harsh environments
1. Approved life jackets must be worn at all times by all persons on board the helicopter, unless an approved survival suit with equivalent buoyancy is worn.
2. Passengers on the helicopter wear survival suits in the following cases:
a) The meteorological bulletin or meteorological forecast available to the helicopter commander shows that the sea water temperature is below +10 °C during the flight;
b) Estimated rescue time exceeds calculated survival time.
3. Requirements for lifeboats:
a) The liferaft boat is installed so that it can be used in sea conditions consistent with the water landing characteristics, buoyancy and center of gravity of the helicopter assessed during the certification process;
b) Life buoys are installed to ensure quick deployment in emergency situations;
c) For helicopters carrying less than 11 people, have at least 01 lifeboat with a rated capacity not less than the maximum number of people on the helicopter;
d) For helicopters carrying 11 or more people, there must be at least two lifeboats with a total capacity sufficient for all people on board the helicopter; In case of loss of 01 lifeboat, the remaining boats have the capacity to accommodate all people on the helicopter;
d) Each lifeboat is equipped with at least 01 emergency locator transmitter;
e) Each lifeboat is equipped with life-saving and life-sustaining means appropriate to the nature of the flight.
4. The helicopter is equipped with an emergency lighting system that uses an independent power source to provide general lighting for the passenger compartment, creating favorable conditions for escape from the helicopter.
5. The helicopter is equipped with an automatically activated emergency locator transmitter capable of simultaneously transmitting signals on frequencies of 121.5 MHz and 406 MHz.
6. Non-removable doors designated as emergency exits for landings on water have a means of securing them in the open position to ensure that the escape of persons on board the helicopter is not impeded in all sea conditions to the maximum extent assessed for landing on water and the buoyancy system.
7. All emergency exits, including crew emergency exits, together with doors, windows or other hatches suitable for use in underwater escape, ensure operability in emergency situations.
8. Based on risk assessment, aircraft operators are allowed to let passengers who are medically incapacitated at an offshore location not wear or only partially wear a life jacket, survival suit or emergency respiratory support system during the return flight or flights between offshore locations."},"translatedText":"Article 1157. Additional procedures and equipment for mining operations in harsh environments

