2.33.1	Objective
This maintenance procedure ensures that all VJC aircraft equipped for Performance-Based Communication and Surveillance (PBCS) operations maintain full compliance with applicable communication and surveillance performance requirements such as RCP240 and RSP180.
2.33.2	Scope
This procedure applies to all maintenance activities related to PBCS equipment and configurations on VJC A330 aircraft used in oceanic and remote airspace operations, where PBCS performance is required.
2.33.3	References
a)	ICAO Doc 9869 (PBCS Manual)
b)	VAR Part 6
c)	CAAV AC 10-021
d)	A330 AFM
e)	VJC Datalink Manual
2.33.4	Responsibilities
a)	The Engineering Director is responsible for ensuring compliance with the PBCS configuration and maintenance program.
b)	The Training Department is responsible for developing PBCS maintenance training programs, and providing the PBCS maintenance training to VJC technical staff, VJC AMO (Aircraft Maintenance Organization), and contracted AMO.
c)	The Technical Quality Assurance (TQA) Department is responsible for reviewing and validating PBCS maintenance records and procedures, and PBCS instructor authorization.
d)	FOE is responsible for updating the MEL items related to PBCS operations.
e)	The contracted AMO is responsible for implementing all PBCS maintenance in accordance with approved procedures.
2.33.5	PBCS System Overview
a)	Performance-Based Communication and Surveillance (PBCS) is a framework that mandates the use of Required Communication Performance (RCP) and Required Surveillance Performance (RSP) specifications.
b)	RCP and RSP specifications are applicable to both Datalink and Voice communication and surveillance systems.
c)	PBCS is particularly relevant for operations in oceanic and remote airspace, where it ensures communication and surveillance systems meet defined performance targets:
1)	RCP240: Communication performance
2)	RSP180: Surveillance performance
d)	In the North Atlantic (NAT) Region, specifically the Minimum Navigation Performance Specification (MNPS) airspace, the following PBCS specifications apply:
1)	RCP240 for Controller-Pilot Datalink Communication (CPDLC)
2)	RSP180 for Automatic Dependent Surveillance – Contract (ADS-C)
e)	An RCP designator (e.g., RCP240) indicates the maximum acceptable time (in seconds) for an Air Traffic Controller to initiate a transaction and receive a response from the flight crew.
Example: RCP240 = 240 seconds
f)	An RSP designator (e.g., RSP180) refers to the maximum acceptable time (in seconds) to deliver surveillance data (such as aircraft position) to the Air Traffic Control (ATC) system.
Example: RSP180 = 180 seconds
g)	According to the VJC A330 Aircraft Flight Manual (AFM), the aircraft’s CPDLC and ADS-C applications have been demonstrated to comply with the aircraft-allocated performance requirements of:
1)	RCP 240 for communication
2)	RSP 180 for surveillance
